1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,550
>> Right now, you're moving around the Sun at about 30 km/s.

2
00:00:05,630 --> 00:00:16,240
>> But the speed isn't steady. In January, Earth moves about 1 km/s faster than in July.

3
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:21,870
>> The rule behind that was found more than 400 years ago, in old records of the planet Mars.

4
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:27,700
And it started with an error of eight arcminutes, which is about a quarter of the width of the Moon.

5
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:31,670
>> A quarter of the Moon? That's all?

6
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:39,090
>> That's all. This is the story of Kepler's second law, the rule of equal areas in equal times.

7
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:49,880
In the late 1500s, the best measurements of the sky came from a Danish nobleman, Tycho Brahe.

8
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:58,950
>> From 1576 he built Uraniborg, on the island of Hven, with the king's money.

9
00:00:59,030 --> 00:01:04,870
>> There were no telescopes yet, so Tycho measured by eye, with large quadrants and sextants.

10
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:12,290
>> His positions came close to one arcminute, which is a sixtieth of a degree.

11
00:01:12,370 --> 00:01:19,130
For scale, the full Moon is about 31 arcminutes across.

12
00:01:19,210 --> 00:01:25,470
>> Year after year, he recorded where the planets were. Mars was among them.

13
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:35,160
>> In 1597 he left Denmark. Soon he was working for the emperor, in Prague.

14
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:41,330
>> And in February 1600, he was joined by a 28-year-old mathematician, Johannes Kepler.

15
00:01:41,410 --> 00:01:48,050
>> Tycho guarded his data, and gave Kepler a single planet, Mars.

16
00:01:48,130 --> 00:01:56,120
>> Then in October 1601, Tycho died after a short illness. Two days later, Kepler took over as imperial mathematician.

17
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:04,380
Tycho's heirs owned the observations, which would delay Kepler's book for years. But Kepler had the Mars data.

18
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:13,880
Now, astronomers had long modelled the planets with circles. Even Copernicus, who put the Sun near the middle, used circles.

19
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:18,850
>> In his case, it was circles riding on circles.

20
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:24,380
>> So Kepler began with a circle too, with the Sun off-centre, and a point from which the motion looks steady.

21
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:27,500
>> That point is called the equant.

22
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:32,730
>> Fitting all that to Tycho's numbers meant one long calculation, over and over again.

23
00:02:32,810 --> 00:02:37,980
He complained in print. "Take pity on me, who had to go through with at least seventy repetitions of it."

24
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:41,350
>> That's seventy times, by hand.

25
00:02:41,430 --> 00:02:48,170
And it nearly worked, matching Tycho's positions to within two arcminutes.

26
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:52,430
>> But at other points in the orbit, it missed by up to eight.

27
00:02:52,510 --> 00:02:55,700
>> Couldn't he call eight close enough?

28
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:00,750
>> Not with Tycho's data. It was far too accurate for an error that size to be ignored.

29
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:07,880
"These eight minutes alone," Kepler wrote, "will lead us along a path to the reform of the whole of Astronomy."

30
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:12,070
>> That's quite a promise, for eight arcminutes.

31
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:17,940
>> Something in his model was wrong, either the circle or the way the planet moved along it.

32
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:27,000
Kepler wanted a physical cause. He imagined a force from the Sun, pushing the planets round, weaker with distance.

33
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:36,600
>> So a planet's speed would fall as its distance grows. Twice as far would mean half as fast.

34
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:41,720
>> But to find where the planet would be, he'd have to add up its distance from the Sun all along the way.

35
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:45,790
>> Every point of the orbit? By hand?

36
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:53,120
>> Exactly. So by late 1602 he'd found a shortcut, which was to measure the area swept by the line from the Sun.

37
00:03:53,200 --> 00:04:00,930
Picture each day's motion as a thin triangle, whose height is the distance from the Sun, and whose base is the distance travelled.

38
00:04:01,010 --> 00:04:07,420
>> They're tall and narrow far away, and short and wide close in.

39
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:13,560
>> And if speed falls as distance grows, every day's triangle has the same area.

40
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:19,840
That's equal areas in equal times. And oddly, Kepler found this second law before the first.

41
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:24,110
>> So the numbers aren't the order he found them in.

42
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:32,350
>> No. And his speed rule only works for orbits that are nearly circles. The area rule works for every orbit.

43
00:04:34,950 --> 00:04:39,890
Now he needed the shape. He tried egg-shaped ovals, again and again.

44
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:41,530
>> How many?

45
00:04:41,610 --> 00:04:47,660
>> About forty failed attempts. Then, in late 1604, he tried an ellipse.

46
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:53,700
>> He'd thought it too simple for earlier astronomers to have missed.

47
00:04:53,780 --> 00:05:00,700
>> But it fitted. He'd even had the formula earlier, without seeing it. "Ah," he wrote, "what a foolish bird I have been!"

48
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:07,670
An ellipse has two special points, its foci. The Sun sits at one. The other is empty.

49
00:05:07,750 --> 00:05:16,210
>> That's Kepler's first law, that the orbit is an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus.

50
00:05:16,290 --> 00:05:21,910
>> Mars's ellipse is barely squashed. It's less than half a percent narrower than it is long.

51
00:05:21,990 --> 00:05:27,340
But the Sun is well off-centre, sitting more than 9% of the way from the middle to the end.

52
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:35,490
>> Only Mercury's orbit is more stretched. He was lucky that his planet was Mars.

53
00:05:35,570 --> 00:05:42,430
>> In 1609 he published it all in a book called Astronomia nova, the New Astronomy.

54
00:05:45,030 --> 00:05:50,790
So where is a planet on a given day? Kepler turned the area rule into an equation.

55
00:05:50,870 --> 00:05:58,660
>> Time gives an angle, M, that ticks round steadily, like a clock hand.

56
00:05:58,740 --> 00:06:05,680
>> The planet's real place is set by another angle, E. And the little e says how stretched the ellipse is.

57
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:12,490
Put together, Kepler's equation says that M equals E, minus e times the sine of E.

58
00:06:12,570 --> 00:06:15,940
>> And you know M, but you want E.

59
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:22,790
>> No algebra can solve it for E. So in 1621, Kepler suggested guessing, then improving the guess.

60
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:30,840
For Mars, start with E equal to M, put each answer back in, and within four rounds the digits stop changing.

61
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:35,660
>> So it's never exact, just ever closer.

62
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:42,150
>> Right. This animation does that for every frame. Here's a stretched orbit, so the effect is easy to see.

63
00:06:42,230 --> 00:06:52,070
>> Split one trip around into twelve equal times, and shade the area each one sweeps.

64
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:58,610
>> Near the Sun, the wedges are short and fat. Far away, they're long and thin.

65
00:06:58,690 --> 00:07:01,030
>> They look nothing alike.

66
00:07:01,110 --> 00:07:06,890
>> But measure them, and each one is a twelfth of the ellipse, or 8.3%.

67
00:07:06,970 --> 00:07:12,240
>> But the distance travelled in each is wildly different.

68
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:20,530
>> On this orbit, the planet moves four times faster at its closest point, perihelion, than at aphelion, its farthest.

69
00:07:23,930 --> 00:07:29,600
>> Earth's orbit is much rounder. Does the rule still hold?

70
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:31,820
>> It does, just more gently.

71
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:38,420
Earth passes perihelion around 3 January, at 147.1 million km from the Sun

72
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:47,370
>> and aphelion around 4 July, at 152.1 million km.

73
00:07:47,450 --> 00:07:56,320
>> So it's fastest in January, about 30.3 km/s, and slowest in July, about 29.3.

74
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:08,670
>> But it shows on the calendar. Getting from the March equinox to the September one takes about 186 days.

75
00:08:08,750 --> 00:08:15,470
>> And the other half of the year takes only about 179, because Earth passes perihelion then, moving faster.

76
00:08:15,550 --> 00:08:20,840
So in the north, spring and summer together last about a week longer than autumn and winter.

77
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:32,410
>> Mars swings more, from 26.5 km/s near the Sun to 22 at the far end.

78
00:08:32,490 --> 00:08:40,400
>> Comets show it best. Halley's Comet dips to about 0.6 AU from the Sun, then out to about 35.

79
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:46,940
>> One AU is Earth's average distance from the Sun.

80
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:48,860
>> So that's out beyond Neptune.

81
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:59,180
>> There, in December 2023, it was crawling along at 0.91 km/s.

82
00:08:59,260 --> 00:09:07,850
>> At the two ends, speed times distance is the same. So near the Sun, it races along at about 54 km/s.

83
00:09:07,930 --> 00:09:14,090
>> It's fifty-nine times closer, so it's fifty-nine times faster.

84
00:09:14,170 --> 00:09:23,090
>> Of its roughly 75-year orbit, it spends only about two years closer to the Sun than Jupiter. It's due back in 2061.

85
00:09:26,290 --> 00:09:31,190
Kepler wasn't done. He wanted a rule that linked all the planets, not just one.

86
00:09:31,270 --> 00:09:38,810
>> By his own account, it came to him on 8 March 1618.

87
00:09:38,890 --> 00:09:45,870
>> Square a planet's time to go round. Cube its distance from the Sun. The ratio is the same for every planet.

88
00:09:45,950 --> 00:09:48,140
>> Try it with Jupiter.

89
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:53,690
>> Jupiter takes 11.86 years to go round. Squared, that's about 141.

90
00:09:53,770 --> 00:10:03,960
>> And it's 5.2 AU out. Cubed, that's about 141 again.

91
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:09,160
>> With Kepler's own numbers, all six known planets agree to within about two percent.

92
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:13,580
>> "I first believed I was dreaming," he wrote.

93
00:10:13,660 --> 00:10:17,140
>> After forty failed ovals, you can see why.

94
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:24,810
>> It's his third law, in his book of 1619, The Harmony of the World.

95
00:10:24,890 --> 00:10:31,960
>> There, Kepler heard music in the orbits. Earth's turning speed, seen from the Sun, varies by about a semitone.

96
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:35,080
>> That's the step from mi to fa.

97
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:42,170
>> "The Earth sings Mi, Fa, Mi," he wrote, and you may hear "misery" and "famine" in the syllables.

98
00:10:42,250 --> 00:10:47,090
In 1621, his Epitome gave the area law its modern form.

99
00:10:47,170 --> 00:10:55,290
>> He also noted that the third law fits Jupiter's four bright moons.

100
00:10:57,890 --> 00:11:02,050
So why should planets obey these rules at all?

101
00:11:02,130 --> 00:11:04,310
>> That's where Isaac Newton comes in.

102
00:11:04,390 --> 00:11:10,760
In 1684 he proved that anything pulled toward a fixed centre sweeps out equal areas in equal times.

103
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:13,810
>> Anything? Not just planets?

104
00:11:13,890 --> 00:11:22,060
>> Anything. His proof, in the Principia of 1687, is beautifully simple. Imagine the pull comes in short, sharp tugs.

105
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:27,850
>> With no pull, the planet goes straight, in equal steps.

106
00:11:27,930 --> 00:11:34,120
>> Each step makes a triangle with the Sun. The same base and the same height give the same area.

107
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:38,740
>> That's equal steps, along one straight line.

108
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:44,600
>> Now a tug toward the Sun bends the path. But it pulls only along the line to the Sun.

109
00:11:44,680 --> 00:11:53,020
>> The new triangle keeps its base and height, so it has the same area again.

110
00:11:53,100 --> 00:11:59,610
>> They share the base from the Sun to B, and the tug slides c parallel to it, so the height stays put.

111
00:11:59,690 --> 00:12:08,880
>> Make the tugs small and frequent, and you get a smooth orbit, with equal areas.

112
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:15,360
>> Notice that the proof never needed to know how strong the pull is. Any pull toward a centre will do.

113
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:20,010
>> Even one that grows with distance, like this?

114
00:12:20,090 --> 00:12:24,360
>> Even that. Today we call this conservation of angular momentum.

115
00:12:24,440 --> 00:12:32,780
The ellipse and the third law need more. They need a pull that weakens with the square of the distance, as gravity does.

116
00:12:35,980 --> 00:12:43,200
Funnily enough, Kepler never called these laws. The word first appeared in print in 1738, in a book by Voltaire.

117
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:48,170
>> And the tidy set of three came in 1804.

118
00:12:48,250 --> 00:12:53,240
>> So, first, a planet's orbit is an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus.

119
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:58,730
>> Second, it sweeps out equal areas in equal times.

120
00:12:58,810 --> 00:13:03,500
>> Third, the period squared goes with the distance cubed.

121
00:13:03,580 --> 00:13:06,840
And it's all because Kepler refused to ignore eight arcminutes.

122
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:10,260
>> That's a quarter of the Moon's width.

123
00:13:10,340 --> 00:13:18,810
>> So this January, when it's coldest in the north, remember that Earth is at its closest to the Sun, and moving fastest.
